{
  "scope_and_limits": [
    {
      "claim": "The study is limited by the lack of a full factorial design, which prevents isolating the effect of the counter-narrative alone or disentangling the impact of the mugshot image from the stigmatizing text.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "First, the ideal version of this survey experiment would have been to utilize a full factorial design (i.e., including the exposure to the counter-narrative perspective arm alone). This was entirely done a result of binding budget constraints... Failing to include the counter-narrative only arm limits the ability to interpret the additional impact of including it with the advertisements",
          "page": 17
        },
        {
          "text": "Additionally, as the advertisement (i.e., Trump’s mugshot with the words ‘convicted criminal’ imposed on the image) was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.",
          "page": 17
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The authors acknowledge that the durability of effects and their replication in less-transparent designs or during active campaign periods remain unknown.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "Relatedly, the extent to which these results would replicate in other contexts and in less-transparent designs is unclear, as is the durability of effects.",
          "page": 17
        },
        {
          "text": "Specifically, the single time-point survey experimental approach is a useful first step, but leaves unanswered several key questions related to the ability of these results to replicate: a) for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative prime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist",
          "page": 17
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The study's reliance on an online panel raises concerns about the potential use of generative AI by respondents, although the platform used has a lower reported rate of bots compared to others.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "It is also incredibly important to acknowledge that this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within the survey.",
          "page": 17
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "The study used a survey experiment with 2,439 self-identified Democrats recruited from the Prolific platform, randomly assigned to three groups: Control, Trump Ad Alone, and Counter-Narrative First.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
          "page": 5
        },
        {
          "text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based research... 2,500 participants were recruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025.",
          "page": 9
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The counter-narrative treatment consisted of an edited excerpt from an article about a formerly incarcerated state representative, designed to provide perspective-getting and highlight redemption.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "The counter-narrative, on the other hand, provided no visual stimulus, but rather an excerpt for participants to read. This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV Legislature?,” by Michael Lyle...",
          "page": 6
        },
        {
          "text": "Importantly, this excerpt included both perspective-getting, where Jackson shared their feelings on the election in their own words, and specifically highlighted the importance of redemption, which has been demonstrated in prior literature to be a key predictor of positive perceptions of individuals with records",
          "page": 6
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The analysis verified randomization by checking for balance in sociodemographic variables across treatment groups, finding no statistically significant differences.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "Given that the methodology utilized was a survey experiment, verifying that there is no evidence of failed randomization is a crucial assumption to check. Table 1 below presents the balance table, reporting proportions of sociodemographic variables across the study sample by treatment group assignment",
          "page": 9
        },
        {
          "text": "There were no statistically significant differences between either the Control and Trump Ad Alone groups, nor the Trump Ad Alone and Counter-Narrative First group. This means that there is no statistical evidence of imbalance between treatment arms that are being compared to one another in this study.",
          "page": 10
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
